{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "description": "Shared acceptance-text digest parity fixture for the browser extension and the Rust runtime. Both languages must accept-trim and whitespace-canonicalize the same text the same way: Rust str::trim()/split_whitespace() Unicode White_Space semantics, with NBSP and BOM folded to a space before the split. JS String.prototype.trim()/\\s must not be used for these digests because they differ on U+FEFF and U+0085. The app_mention_cases section is the second half of the contract: the extension strips only the requested App mention token from the observed user turn, and the runtime accepts exactly that '<App pill><message>' rendering as an explicit expectation instead of trusting a content-script strip. A tolerated case means the observed text is an accepted rendering of the message; an untolerated case must still fail the runtime's expectation set.",
